_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute. Cela n'a aucune importance.\"},{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"During the little prince's departure, the flower apologizes for her prior conduct and calmly reveals that she loves him. She urges him to be happy and to leave, while privately trying not to let him see her cry.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The target contains no undertaking. The flower separately relinquishes the protective globe and tells the little prince to proceed with his already-decided departure. He gives no explicit verbal uptake of the love avowal; instead, he continues expressing concern about the wind and animals.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"I recognize the supplied excerpts as passages from Antoine de Saint-Exupéry's Le Petit Prince. The classification relies only on the supplied text.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence strongly supports an avowal and provides narrative signs of affection, including concealed tears and later descriptions of tenderness. It does not directly settle every aspect of the flower's inner sincerity or the little prince's immediate interpretation of the avowal.\"}"
